  urldate = {2026-05-25},
  abstract = {Integrating wave energy converters (WECs) with mature marine structures is an effective approach to improve the economic viability of multi-type renewable energy generation. This study proposes a novel design of a monopile Offshore Wind Turbine (OWT) integrated with an oscillating water column (OWC) device featuring a horizontal bottom plate, and numerically investigates the hydrodynamic performance of the integrated system. The research examines the influence of incident wave conditions (period and direction), air chamber geometric parameters (front wall draft depth and chamber diameter), and secondary power take-off (PTO) damping simulated by orifices on the system performance. Based on the configuration optimized for the Capture Width Ratio (CWR), an in-depth analysis of the hydrodynamic pressure and load characteristics of the integrated platform is conducted. The results indicate that the peak CWR of the optimized system can reach 1.48. Through the analysis of the wave height field and pressure field in the air chamber, a suction-ejection coupled flow pattern is proposed, which successfully explains the capture of wave energy beyond the width of the air chamber. Even under the most unfavorable incident wave angle of 45\textopenbullet, the wave energy capture efficiency remains at 1.18. Furthermore, the horizontal force acting on the integrated platform is sensitive to the OWC opening ratio, and the horizontal load can be reduced by a maximum of 57\% compared with the platform without OWC integration. A preliminary structural assessment at the prototype scale was also conducted to examine the plausibility of the opening band configuration.},
